\description{
Matches plant names, exactly or with fuzzy matching, against a static copy of the
World Flora Online (WFO) taxonomic backbone, and replaces synonyms by their accepted
names. Names are given either as full names (column \code{spec.name}) or as separate genus,
species and infraspecific columns.
}
\details{
\strong{Preparation of names.} When \code{spec.name} is used, names are first cleaned in this
order: \code{squish}, \code{spec.name.tolower}, \code{spec.name.sub}, \code{spec.name.nobrackets},
\code{spec.name.nonumber}; finally \code{" x "} is read as hybrid notation and replaced by
\code{" ×"}. The submitted names are kept in column \code{spec.name.ORIG}, and columns
\code{Squished}, \code{Brackets.detected} and \code{Number.detected} show what was changed.

\strong{Matching.} Names are matched in this order, each step only for names that the
previous steps did not match:
\enumerate{
\item exact matches (hybrid notation does not matter: "Mentha x piperita", "Mentha
×piperita" and "Mentha × piperita" are the same name);
\item conserved alternative family names (Compositae, Leguminosae, Umbelliferae,
Palmae, Cruciferae, Guttiferae, Labiatae, Gramineae) not in the backbone, matched
with their standard names (Asteraceae, Fabaceae, ...);
\item fuzzy matches of the full name with \code{\link[=agrep]{agrep()}}, then of its first two words
(\code{Fuzzy.two}), checked by \code{Fuzzy.epithet.check} (optional);
\item genus then epithet (\code{Fuzzy.genus}, optional), also when the fuzzy matches of the previous
step all belong to unrelated genera; if the submitted genus exists in the backbone and
these matches are all in other families, they are not accepted ("Schizolobium
robustum" is not matched with \emph{Schizoglossum robustum}, Apocynaceae) but listed in
\code{Fuzzy.alternatives};
\item same epithet in another genus of the family (\code{Fuzzy.transfer}, optional);
\item the genus only (\code{Fuzzy.one}; restricted by the optional \code{Fuzzy.one.strict}).
}
Fuzzy candidates are reduced by \code{Fuzzy.within}, \code{Fuzzy.min} or \code{Fuzzy.shortest}.
Submitted names with several matches get several rows, numbered by \code{Subseq}; \code{\link[=WFO.one]{WFO.one()}}
then keeps one row per submitted name.

\strong{What fuzzy matching cannot do.} \code{\link[=agrep]{agrep()}} finds the submitted name \emph{within}
longer names, which is how incomplete names such as "Pygeum afric" are found, but it can
also produce matches for local names ("Palo blanco" is within two edits of part of
"Tribulago blancoi"); such matches have a large \code{Fuzzy.dist} compared with the length of
the name. Local names that are themselves names in the backbone (such as the genus
\emph{Guayaba}) are matched like any other name. Review fuzzy matches, and remove local names
before matching where possible.

\strong{WFO releases.} Both the conventions of WFO releases up to 2025 (ranks "SPECIES"
and "var.", hybrids written "Mentha ×piperita") and those of the WFO Plant List
2026-06 onwards (ranks "species" and "variety", hybrids written "Mentha × piperita")
are understood, for example in \code{exclude.infraspecific} and when matching by
\code{Genus}/\code{Species}/\code{Infraspecific.rank} columns.

\strong{Speed.} The backbone is indexed once per call, and fuzzy matching is done once
per distinct name. Before each \code{\link[=agrep]{agrep()}} search, an exact prefilter removes names that
cannot match: with at most \emph{k} edits, at least one of \emph{k} + 1 pieces of the
submitted name occurs unchanged in every match (pigeonhole principle), so a fast
fixed-string search for the pieces leaves few candidates. Results are identical to
searching the whole backbone.

\strong{Differences with WorldFlora 1.14-5} are listed in the NEWS file: notably,
\code{Unique} is \code{FALSE} for names with several exact matches, and conserved family names and
unmatched names are no longer replaced by other records. Results are
unchanged otherwise: by default the matching steps, result columns and their formats are
those of WorldFlora (the optional steps \code{Fuzzy.genus}, \code{Fuzzy.transfer},
\code{Fuzzy.one.strict} and \code{Fuzzy.epithet.check} are switched off).
}
\examples{
## ---- default settings (those of WorldFlora) ----
# WFO.example2026 is a small extract of the WFO Plant List 2026-06
data(WFO.example2026)
m <- WFO.match(c("Faidherbia albida", "Acacia albida", "Pygeum afric", "Mentha x piperita",
                 "Prunus sp."), WFO.data = WFO.example2026)
m[, c("spec.name", "Subseq", "Fuzzy", "Fuzzy.dist", "scientificName",
      "scientificNameAuthorship", "Old.name")]
# one match per name, with the default criteria of WFO.one()
WFO.one(m)[, c("spec.name", "scientificName", "scientificNameAuthorship", "One.Reason")]

# names in separate columns
WFO.match(data.frame(Genus = "Gambeya", Species = "boiviniana", Infraspecific.rank = "",
                     Infraspecific = ""), WFO.data = WFO.example2026)$scientificName

## ---- non-default settings, marked "non-default" ----
# homonyms of "Acacia albida": the authors decide which one was meant
aa <- data.frame(spec.name = "Acacia albida", Authorship = c("Delile", "Lindl.", ""))
m <- WFO.match(aa, WFO.data = WFO.example2026,
               verbose = FALSE)                               # non-default: no messages
WFO.one(m, Auth.dist = "Auth.dist", Old.author.dist = "Old.author.dist",   # non-default
        verbose = FALSE)[, c("Authorship", "scientificName", "Old.name", "One.Reason")]

# matching steps of WorldFloraFast for difficult names
x <- c("Reinwardtiod cinerum",            # truncated genus
       "Chrysophyllum madagascariensis",  # species transferred to another genus
       "Palma colorada")                  # local name
m <- WFO.match(x, WFO.data = WFO.example2026, verbose = FALSE,
               Fuzzy.genus = TRUE, Fuzzy.transfer = TRUE,             # non-default
               Fuzzy.one.strict = TRUE, Fuzzy.epithet.check = TRUE)   # non-default
m[, c("spec.name", "Matched", "Fuzzy.genus", "Fuzzy.transfer", "scientificName", "Old.name")]
# with the default settings, these names are matched with genera only
WFO.one(WFO.match(x, WFO.data = WFO.example2026, verbose = FALSE))$scientificName

# all fuzzy candidates, then one match per name
e1 <- WFO.match("Omalanthus populneus", WFO.data = WFO.example2026,
                Fuzzy.min = FALSE, verbose = FALSE)            # non-default
e1[, c("Subseq", "Fuzzy.dist", "scientificName", "taxonomicStatus", "Old.name")]
WFO.one(e1, verbose = FALSE)[, c("spec.name", "scientificName", "One.Reason")]

# names in capitals
WFO.match("FAIDHERBIA ALBIDA", WFO.data = WFO.example2026,
          spec.name.tolower = TRUE, verbose = FALSE)$scientificName   # non-default

# exclusion of infraspecific matches: 0 rows, the variety is excluded
nrow(WFO.match("Gambeya boiviniana var. lavanchiana", WFO.data = WFO.example2026,
               acceptedNameUsageID.match = FALSE, exclude.infraspecific = TRUE,   # non-default
               verbose = FALSE))
\dontrun{
WFO.download()                    # complete backbone, available as WFO.data
WFO.match(c("Acacia nilotica", "Coffea arabika"), WFO.data = WFO.data,
          cores = 4)                                         # non-default
}
}
